import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';

const person = `{
  "name": "Asha",
  "active": true,
  "skills": ["HTML", "CSS"],
  "projects": 3,
  "manager": null
}`;
const invalid = `{
  name: 'Asha',
  "skills": ["HTML", "CSS",],
  // the last skill is new
}`;
const valid = `{
  "name": "Asha",
  "skills": ["HTML", "CSS"]
}`;
const xml = `<person>
  <name>Asha</name>
  <active>true</active>
  <skills><skill>HTML</skill><skill>CSS</skill></skills>
</person>`;
const json = `{
  "name": "Asha",
  "active": true,
  "skills": ["HTML", "CSS"]
}`;
---

<Guide
  title="What is JSON? Format, files, examples and how to open them | thetoolproject"
  description="Learn what JSON stands for, how its format works, what .json files contain, how to open and create one, and how JSON compares with XML."
  path="/guides/json/"
  heading="What is JSON? A practical guide to the format and its files"
  updated="2026-09-24"
>
  <p slot="intro" class="guide-intro">JSON is a plain-text way to represent data. If you can read a list of names and values, you can read the basics of JSON. This guide walks through a real example, the punctuation, and the everyday questions about .json files.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-json">What is JSON, and what does JSON stand for?</h2>
  <p><strong>JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.</strong> It is a text format for exchanging structured data between programs. Its name comes from JavaScript, but JSON is language-independent: applications written in many languages can read and write it.</p>
  <p>JSON stores values inside objects and arrays. An <strong>object</strong> gives values names, such as <code>"name"</code> or <code>"active"</code>. An <strong>array</strong> holds values in order, such as a list of skills. A complete JSON document can also be a single string, number, boolean, or <code>null</code>, though objects and arrays are the most common files you will encounter.</p>
  <p class="example-label">A small JSON document</p>
  <pre><code>{person}</code></pre>
  <p>Here, <code>"name"</code> is a key and <code>"Asha"</code> is its string value. <code>true</code> is a boolean, <code>3</code> is a number, <code>null</code> means there is no value, and the values inside <code>[ ]</code> form an array.</p>

  <h2 id="json-file">What is a JSON file?</h2>
  <p>A <strong>JSON file</strong>, often called a <strong>.json file</strong>, is a text file containing JSON data. The <code>.json</code> extension identifies its format; it does not require a special program to read the text. A file named <code>person.json</code> might contain the example above.</p>
  <p>JSON files are common in app settings, data exports, and information passed between a website and an API. The file is intended for programs to parse, but its text is usually readable by people too. Pretty printing adds line breaks and indentation for people; it does not change the data.</p>

  <h2 id="json-format">What is JSON format?</h2>
  <p>JSON format is a small set of rules for writing values. The marks carry meaning:</p>
  <table class="guide-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Mark</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>{'{ }'}</code></td><td>An object with named members</td><td><code>{'{"age": 3}'}</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[ ]</code></td><td>An ordered array of values</td><td><code>["HTML", "CSS"]</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>:</code></td><td>Separates an object key from its value</td><td><code>"age": 3</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>,</code></td><td>Separates members or array items</td><td><code>[1, 2]</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>" "</code></td><td>Surrounds keys and string values</td><td><code>"name"</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Values can be strings, numbers, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, objects, or arrays. Object keys must be strings in double quotes. Strings also use double quotes. JSON permits spaces, tabs, and line breaks around structural marks, so both one-line and indented forms can be valid.</p>
  <p>The square brackets in <code>"skills": ["HTML", "CSS"]</code> mean the <code>skills</code> value is a list. See the <a href="/guides/json-syntax-square-brackets/">square brackets guide</a> for empty, nested, and mixed arrays, plus common bracket errors.</p>

  <h2 id="uses">What is JSON used for?</h2>
  <p>Programs use JSON when they need to share data in a predictable shape. A website might receive a product list from an API, an app might save preferences in a <code>settings.json</code> file, or a service might export records for another tool. The same object-and-array structure can represent a single record or thousands of records.</p>
  <p>JSON describes data rather than its visual presentation. If you want to show that data on a web page, you can <a href="/json-to-html/">convert JSON to an HTML table or list</a>.</p>
  <p>For spreadsheet imports, you can <a href="/guides/json-to-csv/">convert JSON to CSV</a>, with separate steps for the browser tool, Excel, and Python.</p>

  <aside class="guide-tool" aria-labelledby="try-json-tool">
    <h2 id="try-json-tool">See your JSON as a table</h2>
    <p>Paste a JSON object or array into our converter, choose Table or List, and preview the HTML in your browser.</p>
    <a href="/json-to-html/">Open JSON to HTML converter</a>
