  <h2 id="open-json">How to open a JSON file</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the file ending in <code>.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Open it with a text editor, such as Notepad on Windows, TextEdit in plain-text mode on macOS, or a code editor. You can usually right-click the file and choose <strong>Open with</strong>.</li>
    <li>Read or edit the text. If it is all on one line, use a code editor’s Format Document command to indent it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You can also open many <code>.json</code> files in a web browser to read them. To work with the data as a table, choose the file in the <a href="/json-to-html/">JSON to HTML converter</a>. Treat JSON files from unknown sources as text; there is no reason to run a program just to inspect one.</p>

  <h2 id="create-json">How to create a JSON file</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open a plain-text editor and type a valid JSON value. Start with <code>{'{ }'}</code> for named fields or <code>[ ]</code> for a list.</li>
    <li>Put object keys and text values in double quotes. Add commas <em>between</em> items, never after the last item.</li>
    <li>Save as UTF-8 text with a <code>.json</code> extension, such as <code>person.json</code>. In Notepad, choose <strong>All files</strong> if needed so the name does not become <code>person.json.txt</code>.</li>
    <li>Check that a JSON parser or editor accepts it. Our converter will report common syntax errors with a line and column.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="example-label">A common mistake, followed by valid JSON</p>
  <pre><code>{invalid}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{valid}</code></pre>
  <p>The first version has an unquoted key, single-quoted text, a trailing comma, and a comment. The second version fixes all four.</p>

  <h2 id="comments">How to comment in JSON</h2>
  <p><strong>Standard JSON has no comment syntax.</strong> Neither <code>//</code> nor <code>/* ... */</code> is allowed in a JSON document. If a file accepts those marks, the program may be using a JSON-like extension rather than standard JSON.</p>
  <p>If you control the data format and need a note, you can add a normal field such as <code>"note": "Shown to editors only"</code>. That field is real data, so only do this when the receiving program allows it. Otherwise keep the explanation in a separate README or documentation file.</p>

  <h2 id="xml-vs-json">What is the difference between XML and JSON?</h2>
  <p>Both formats can exchange structured information. JSON uses objects, arrays, and typed values. XML uses nested elements marked with opening and closing tags, and it can also attach attributes to elements. The same simple record could look like this:</p>
  <p class="example-label">JSON</p><pre><code>{json}</code></pre>
  <p class="example-label">XML</p><pre><code>{xml}</code></pre>
  <table class="guide-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">JSON</th><th scope="col">XML</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>How is a list shown?</td><td>An array inside <code>[ ]</code></td><td>Repeated elements, such as multiple <code>&lt;skill&gt;</code> tags</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How are values typed?</td><td>Strings, numbers, booleans, and <code>null</code> have JSON syntax</td><td>Element text is text unless an application or schema assigns a type</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How is structure marked?</td><td>Braces, brackets, keys, and commas</td><td>Tags, attributes, and nesting</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Choose the format the receiving system expects. JSON is common for web APIs and app configuration; XML remains useful where a document or integration already relies on XML elements, attributes, or schema rules.</p>

  <h2 id="quick-answers">Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>What are JSON files?</h3>
  <p>They are plain-text files containing data written in JSON format, typically named with the <code>.json</code> extension.</p>
  <h3>Is a .json file the same as JSON?</h3>
  <p>JSON is the format. A <code>.json</code> file is one way to store text in that format. JSON can also travel directly in an API response without a file.</p>
  <h3>Can I open .json files without coding?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Open the file in a text editor to read it, or use a viewer or converter to display its structure.</p>

  <div class="guide-sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>The format rules above follow <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259">RFC 8259, The JavaScript Object Notation Data Interchange Format</a> and <a href="https://ecma-international.org/publications-and-standards/standards/ecma-404/">ECMA-404, The JSON data interchange syntax</a>. XML structure follows the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/">W3C XML 1.0 specification</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Guide>
